# Unit Testing Standards

## Co-location

Tests ALWAYS live next to the file they test, never in a separate `test/` tree:

```
src/modules/strategy/strategy.service.ts
src/modules/strategy/strategy.service.spec.ts   # ✅ same folder
```

Name the spec file `<original-filename>.spec.ts`. Jest already picks these up via `testRegex` in `package.json` — no per-module config needed.

## Test Setup

USE `Test.createTestingModule` from `@nestjs/testing` with fully mocked providers — NEVER let a unit test hit a real database or external service:

```typescript
const module: TestingModule = await Test.createTestingModule({
  providers: [
    StrategyService,
    { provide: PrismaService, useValue: { strategy: { findMany: jest.fn() } } },
  ],
}).compile();
```

For classes with no DI container involvement (guards, strategies), instantiate directly with mocked constructor args instead of bootstrapping a module.

## Mocking

- Type mocks as `jest.Mocked<SomeService>` and cast plain mock objects with `as unknown as jest.Mocked<SomeService>` — never use `any`.
- `PrismaService` and other injected services are ALWAYS mocked in unit tests — real DB access belongs in e2e tests only (out of scope for `*.spec.ts`).
- Imports follow the same rules as source code: use `@src/*` for cross-module imports, relative imports for same-folder files.

## Scope per module

For each module aim to cover: services (business logic, error paths), controllers (response shape, guard usage is exercised via e2e not unit), and any custom guards/strategies with actual logic. Thin wrapper classes with no logic of their own (e.g. `class FooGuard extends AuthGuard('foo') {}`) only need a smoke test confirming they're wired correctly — don't force elaborate behavior tests on them.

## Running tests

Use `npm run test`, `npm run test:watch`, or `npm run test:cov` — never invoke `jest` directly.
